+2.2 If $\ell>k+1$ and $\widehat v$ is real-valued, choose the Bramble–Hilbert polynomial $q\in\mathbb P_k$ from [F3]. If $\widehat v$ is complex-valued, apply the second clause of [F3] with $r=k+1$, $s=\ell$ to its real and imaginary parts, obtaining $q_R,q_I\in\mathbb P_k$, and set $q=q_R+iq_I$. In either case $q$ is reproduced by $\widehat I$. Then [F3] bounds the $W^{\ell,p}$ norm of $\widehat v-q$ by $C\sum_{n=k+1}^{\ell}|\widehat v|_{W^{n,p}}$; because $m\le k+1<\ell$, the same controls its $W^{m,p}$ norm. For complex $\widehat v$, [F8] combines the component estimates with at most a fixed change in $C$. Step 1.1 gives $$|\widehat v-\widehat I\widehat v|_{W^{m,p}} \le C\sum_{n=k+1}^{\ell}|\widehat v|_{W^{n,p}}.$$ [F3, F7, F8, step 1.1]
 
